WEBVTT

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:07.990
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. Let's introduce vectors

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:16.990
in Verilog. To recap, we have learned about ViR, and we use ViR in assignment statements

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:23.190
and in module instantiations. Also, when we were declaring our modules, all the input

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.710
and output ports by default were wires.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.430
We also used wired in a continuous assignment statement

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.470
like three statements shown here.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:40.470
And only when any of the values on the right-hand side

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.270
changes the expression on the right-hand side

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.110
get re-evaluated and the variable of type of wire

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.910
on the left-hand side gets new value.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:58.910
bearing in mind that these continuous assignment statements do not execute sequentially.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:03.910
Also, in our test benches we used registers.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:12.910
Register integers time and real time are new types of variables that are in the same group.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:18.910
However, only register and integers are synthesizable.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.710
Once it's assigned, register keeps its value.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.630
So far, we used it in the test bench.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.870
You remember that for all input ports,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.190
we created variable of type of register.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:35.270
And within initial statement, we assigned their value.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.270
So, in zero underscore tv held the value zero.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.710
We waited for 100 units of time.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.390
And after that, we assign new value this.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:54.390
So basically, a register retain the value assigned to it,

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.550
variable of type of register retain that value.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.550
It may be synthesized.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.310
Basically, it might represent in your design

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.750
a real physical storage such as register.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.430
However, for example, in this test bench here,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.430
it is going to be just a variable

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.230
that keeps its value, it's not going to be synthesized.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.830
They mind you, test matches are not synthesized.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.330
Variables are used in procedural assignment statements.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.090
And procedural assignment statements,

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.830
as you will see in the following topic,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.850
do execute in sequence.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.810
One thing, so we are going to learn,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.810
We already have learned about initial blocks

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.730
and always blocks.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:49.250
As you remember in our test benches,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.170
initial blocks were specified and executed

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.490
only once in simulations.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.450
They are not synthesized.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.670
Always blocks are a little bit different than that

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.970
and we will talk about them in the next topic.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:10.410
Both vias and registers can be scalars

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:15.410
scalars, which is basically having bit width of one,

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.610
or they have two, three, or n width of bits.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:27.610
You see here on the left-hand side, several examples.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.930
They are basically very similar to each other.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.730
We've here declare a wire.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.410
It's called one underscore bit underscore W.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.130
And this is basically one bit or scalar.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:49.130
We can make two bit, three bit and bit.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.690
It's very important to know that you always have

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:57.690
the dimensions that are specified between keyword wire

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.170
and the name of the variable.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:07.170
and it is always n minus one to zero for n-bit a vector.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.230
You can have them in wires,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:15.140
you can have them in registers, and as seen here.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.980
When using vectors, we need to know

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:22.980
that you can access each single bit of the vector.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.940
So for example, here, for a signed statement,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.580
we have two bits, sorry, one bit,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.220
And when we assign, we can assign directly that one bit

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.060
to this.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.380
For two-bit, we are showing here

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.740
that we are assigning both bits here.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.060
But for three-bit wire, as seen here,

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.600
we are assigning basically one by one.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.600
We can access and use or assigned two,

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.260
and this is an example of assigning two,

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.600
on each bit separately.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.620
Basically, those three statements

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.920
are exactly the same as the statement here on the bottom

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.360
where variable three underscore bit underscore W

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.860
is assigned all three bits at one same instance.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.660
And here the variables bit two, bit one and bit zero

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:22.540
are assigned separately.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:27.540
Again, we can not just access single bit

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.020
but we can also select part of vector.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.680
I read, let's look at the example on the left-hand side here

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.560
when we have declared a variable of 16 bits,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.000
so 15 to zero, 16-bit bus.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.400
And then we can assign lower 8-bit

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.680
and upper 8-bit separately.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.480
On the right-hand side,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:57.200
there is a assignment of 3-bit wire,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.160
similar like we did previously,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.760
but we use concatenate operation.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.840
So for this concatenate operation,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.280
we decided and the curly braces are the ones

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.760
that are showing that we are doing concatenation.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:14.960
So most significant bit is always going to be zero.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.400
Next bit, we are going to grab

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.760
entire value of one underscore bit underscore w,

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.760
and the least significant bit is going to be basically

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.760
basically two underscore bit underscore W,

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.060
and you're just going to grab bit two out of that vector.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.820
So basically we can isolate one bit

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.140
or part of the vector,

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.300
and we can access that,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.620
that can be on the left-hand side of expression.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.620
So we can assign to a single bit or two part of vector,

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.540
Or this can be on the right-hand side of an expression

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.940
and grab that value to be assigned with something else.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.840
And I hope this was helpful.

